From 77f6a6cea7005f4528bc03402773c541cdb8f407 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 19:43:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer: play Dolby Vision that comes in two video tracks
 of MP4 and Matroska files

Dolby Vision profile 7 in two video tracks was joined only from a
transport stream. Two kinds of files that people play have the same two
tracks in other containers, and they played HDR10:

- Matroska files that keep the two tracks of a UHD Blu-ray (issue 5:
  "DT" files play HDR10, "ST" ones Dolby Vision);
- MP4 files with the dual-track carriage of Dolby's specification
  ("Dolby Vision Streams Within the ISO Base Media File Format", Annex
  C: a 'dvhe' or 'dvh1' enhancement track with a 'vdep' reference to the
  base track). The file of issue 1 ("FEL TEST DT DL P7 CMV4.0 4000nits
  V4") is one: its base track is 'hev1' with an empty hvcC record, which
  is why the log said "no sequence parameter set in the extradata", and
  it has no "DV:" line because the base track has no Dolby Vision record
  of its own. The record, profile 7 with both layers and the RPU, is on
  its enhancement track.

libavformat 9.0 already groups both: an MP4 enhancement track by its
reference, its sample entry and its record; a Matroska one, which has no
reference, by its record when there is exactly one other HEVC track. The
join takes them now, with what differs from a transport stream:

- the units have a length before them (hvcC), not start codes. The units
  of the enhancement access unit are appended in the framing of the base
  packet (the length size of each track from its record), wrapped in
  type 63 as before, the RPU as it is. A unit of type 63 in an
  enhancement access unit is refused: no enhancement layer has one, and
  a track that has them is a one-track stream that the Matroska grouping
  could take for a layer;
- Matroska gives no decoding times. libavformat makes some up for each
  track from its own reordering, which differs between the layers (in
  the test stream the base layer is coded in its presentation order and
  the enhancement layer is not), so they do not pair: the first
  enhancement unit, the random-access one, had no time and was dropped,
  and every base access unit went with the enhancement unit of the
  access unit after it (2874 of 2875). The time by which
  two packets pair is made from the presentation times instead: each
  track presents one picture at each time, so from a random-access
  picture of a track on, the n-th of its times in order belongs to the
  n-th access unit from there in both tracks. It is known once 16 more
  packets of the track came (HEVC allows 15 pictures to be decoded ahead
  of one that they follow). Packets of a track before its first
  random-access picture (after a seek) have no time; base packets do not
  wait for an enhancement layer that brought SetSilent() packets without
  one;
- each MP4 track has its own time base: the decoding times of the
  enhancement track are taken to the one of the base track;
- a Matroska enhancement record in use says that the track has a base
  layer (libavformat ignores that bit for the same reason); MP4 and
  transport-stream records must say it has none, as before;
- libavformat pairs Matroska tracks by their records only: in MP4 and
  Matroska the enhancement track must also have the size of an
  enhancement layer of the base track (a quarter, or the same up to
  1920x1080), or the pair is not joined;
- the parameter sets of the enhancement layer are in the hvcC record of
  its track, and in a 'dvh1' track nowhere else: the decoder of the layer
  gets that record (doviELConfig), as it gets the one of the enhancement
  layer of a one-track MP4 file.

A transport stream is joined as before, to the byte. When anything does
not fit (another container, another size, other framing, no record), the
two tracks are left as they were and the base layer plays as HDR10, as
it did.

Measured with the host tests (dvmerge_test.cpp and fel_tracks_test.c, run
with FFmpeg 9.0.2 on the files themselves): the profile 7 FEL test stream
in Matroska and in MP4 (both made from its transport stream with
FFmpeg's muxers; the MP4 given its reference and sample entry), with an
enhancement record that says "base layer" and as 'dvh1', and the file of
issue 1 in MP4, Matroska and a transport stream with the stream numbers
of a disc:

- every joined access unit has the same base units, the same enhancement
  units and RPU, and the same time of its enhancement unit as the
  transport stream gives (2875 of 2875 in Matroska and MP4; 2908 of 2908
  for the file of issue 1, which joins every packet);
- after five seeks, every joined packet is the one of the whole read;
- through the enhancement-layer module, every picture gets the same
  enhancement picture, by its samples, as from the transport stream
  (2875 pictures), and that is the one the stream presents at its time;
- the transport stream as 188-byte packets, with its descriptor and as
  a disc, gives the same join as the BDAV stream;
- the same two tracks where nothing declares them (a transport stream
  with the stream numbers of a disc but no Blu-ray program, one with
  other stream numbers, Matroska without the record, MP4 without the
  reference) are not joined: the base layer plays as before;
- the old code joins none of the MP4 and Matroska files; ten mutations
  of the new code (each takes out one part of it) fail the test, and
  without the record of a 'dvh1' enhancement track the module pairs 28
  of 2875 pictures (none with another picture's layer).

Not tested: a file written by MakeMKV (none was at hand: the Matroska
files are made with FFmpeg's muxer), and playback itself, on a GPU and a
TV.
